{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-141577","model_call":74,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1837"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1837"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_414826521#1_997086581","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1837","rank":3,"snippet":"able to fly a house with balloons is pretty preposterous. We're not physicists, but one of our technical directors calculated that it would take on the order of 20- to 30 million balloons to lift Carl's house. We ended up using 10,297 for most of the floating scenes, and 20,622 when it actually lifts off. The number varies from shot to shot, depending on the angle, the distance, and fine tuning the size so that it feels interesting, believable and visually simple. The Physics in Disney/Pixar's \"Up\" (Physics and Physicists blog) When a couple of people from a <cover>retirement home came to pick up Carl, he handed his bags to them and told them to wait for him while he said goodbye to his house.\" , \"start_char\": 602.000000 , \"title\": \"Change of Subject: The physics of `Up' -- how many balloons </cover>would it really take to lift a house?\" , \"url\": \"https://blogs.chicagotribune.com/news_columnists_ezorn/2009/06/the-physics-of-up-how-many-balloons-would-it-really-take-to-lift-a-house.html\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_31_369552630#5_792749747","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":9},"model_calls":74,"document_judgments":30,"relevance_3":19,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
